# Novium Ownership Patterns for AI Code Generation

## Primitives Copy

```novium
let x: int = 42
let y: int = x  // y is a copy; x still valid after this line
print(${x})  // OK: x still valid
print(${y})  // OK: y is independent
```

## Complex Types Move by Default

```novium
let s: string = "hello"
// Without explicit ownership, string moves into function
let process: fn(string) void = fn(s: string) void:
    print(${s})

process(s)  // s moved INTO process; CAN'T use s after this line
// print(${s})  // COMPILE ERROR: s no longer valid
```

## Fix: Clone if Need to Keep Original

```novium
let s: string = "hello"
let s2: string = s.clone()  // s2 is copy; s still valid
process(s)  // s moved, but s2 still valid
```

## Fix: Borrow Instead

```novium
let s: string = "hello"
let s_ref: &string = &s  // Borrow; s still valid
process(*s_ref)  // Dereference and pass; s still valid after
```

## own Keyword for Explicit Transfer

```novium
// Explicitly transfer ownership
let s: string = "data"
fn process(own s: string) void:
    print(${s})

process(own s)  // s transferred; CAN'T use after this line
// OR:
// process(own s.clone())  // Clone first, then transfer clone
```

## Summary Rules

| Situation | Action |
|-----------|--------|
| Primitives (int, float, bool) | Copy automatically |
| Complex types (string, vector, struct) | Move by default |
| Need to keep original | Use `.clone()` or `&` borrow |
| Want explicit transfer | Use `own` keyword |
| Just need to read | Use `&` reference |